Non-Occupational Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (nPEP) Assessment
General Practice Other
High-risk HIV exposure within 72 hours, High patient anxiety, 28-day antiretroviral prescription, Baseline HIV testing

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-029
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychological distress and urgent request for prophylactic medication following a high-risk sexual exposure occurring exactly 60 hours ago.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a highly distressed patient reporting a recent, high-risk exposure to HIV. The critical, time-sensitive window for initiating non-occupational post-exposure prophylaxis (nPEP) is 72 hours. The patient is currently at 60 hours post-exposure and was unable to access an emergency department or sexual health clinic today. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to evaluate the transmission risk, perform an immediate baseline medical assessment, and prescribe the 28-day antiretroviral regimen before the definitive 72-hour efficacy window closed permanently. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have rendered the prophylaxis completely ineffective, resulting in a lifelong, preventable HIV infection.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "The condom broke two and a half days ago with someone I don't know, I'm terrified I've caught HIV, and I know I only have hours left to get the pills."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents following a recent high-risk exposure to human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) through condomless sexual intercourse (receptive anal). The presentation is occurring strictly within 72 hours of the exposure event (currently at 60 hours). The patient is experiencing extreme high anxiety and psychological distress regarding potential seroconversion. An assessment of the source individual's known or suspected HIV status reveals they are a casual partner of unknown serostatus and uncontactable. An evaluation of the specific exposure type confirms a high transmission probability. The patient demands immediate pharmaceutical intervention and is severely panicked about the closing time window. They report no acute systemic symptoms, fevers, or rashes today.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently pacing, tearful, and highly fixated on the elapsed time.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety; risk of catastrophic psychological decompensation if the medication window is missed.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/92 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Highly anxious, frequently checking the time on their phone, diaphoretic.
Physical Exam: General: No acute signs of seroconversion illness (no generalized lymphadenopathy, no pharyngitis, no maculopapular rash). The clinical pathway mandates baseline testing for other sexually transmitted infections including syphilis, gonorrhoea, and chlamydia (to be drawn immediately via pathology form). The primary intervention is the urgent prescription of a 28-day course of a three-drug antiretroviral regimen. Expected investigation results include a negative baseline fourth-generation HIV antigen/antibody test ensuring the patient is not already chronically infected prior to initiating prophylaxis, which will be drawn tomorrow morning.
Environmental Observations: Safe, private home environment suitable for a highly confidential sexual health consultation.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; denies any history of renal impairment.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Non-Occupational Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (nPEP) Assessment - Status: Stable (Physiologically)
Differentials:

Acute Retroviral Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The extremely short timeframe (60 hours) since exposure; acute seroconversion illness typically manifests 2 to 4 weeks post-exposure, not immediately.

Established HIV Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient reports a negative HIV test 3 months prior, though baseline serology is required to definitively rule this out before commencing the 28-day regimen.

Other Acute STIs (e.g., Gonorrhea) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of purulent urethral/rectal discharge or dysuria at this highly acute stage, though comprehensive screening is required.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High individual infectious risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a high-risk mucosal exposure to HIV and presented at 60 hours post-exposure, dangerously close to the absolute 72-hour clinical cutoff for nPEP efficacy. Telephone triage accurately identified this as a critical, time-sensitive infectious disease emergency. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly mandatory to formally risk-stratify the exposure, rule out medical contraindications to antiretrovirals, and immediately prescribe the triple-therapy regimen. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would have caused the patient to exceed the 72-hour window, completely eliminating the chance to prevent a lifelong, incurable retroviral infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a formal HIV risk assessment confirming eligibility for nPEP. Prescribed a 28-day course of Tenofovir disoproxil/Emtricitabine 300/200mg daily AND Dolutegravir 50mg daily. Provided intensive counselling regarding strict, absolute adherence to the daily dosing schedule. Discussed potential medication side effects including nausea, headache, and fatigue. Generated urgent pathology requests for baseline HIV serology, Syphilis, Hepatitis B/C, and comprehensive STI screening.
Positive Behavioral Change: To guarantee flawless adherence to this critical 28-day medication course, set a daily, recurring alarm on your phone for a specific time you are always awake and at home (e.g., 8:00 PM). Label the alarm "Life Saving Meds." Immediately upon the alarm sounding, take the tablets and leave the pill bottles upside down on your bedside table until the next morning. This provides an immediate, undeniable visual confirmation that the dose was taken, aggressively preventing missed doses and ensuring maximum viral suppression.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or Sexual Health Clinic review strictly within 3 days for baseline blood draw, side effect review, and ongoing support.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop a severe, widespread blistering skin rash, sudden yellowing of your eyes (jaundice), or severe, unrelenting vomiting that prevents you from keeping the antiretroviral tablets down.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The medication must be taken for exactly 28 days to be effective. You may feel nauseous or tired for the first few days; do not stop taking the medication under any circumstances.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
If you vomit within 2 hours of taking your dose, you must immediately take another full dose to ensure the medication is absorbed.
